  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Which of the following describes the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{z}^{2}-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}?$",
  "worked_solution": "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{z}^{2}-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} & =c \\\\ \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & ={c}^{2} \\\\ {c}^{2}{z}^{2}-{c}^{2}{x}^{2}-{c}^{2}{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {c}^{2}{x}^{2}+{c}^{2}{y}^{2}-{c}^{2}{z}^{2} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)$ can't be zero or negative. Therefore, we get a family of hyperboloids of two sheets:\n\n$$\n{c}^{2}{x}^{2}+{c}^{2}{y}^{2}-{c}^{2}{z}^{2}=-1,\\,c\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$",
